<section xml:id="solving-quadratic-inequalities">
  <title>Solving Quadratic Inequalities</title>

  <p>
    In year one we learnt about quadratic functions, how to solve them
    algebraically and how to illustrate them graphically. Remember that
    quadratic functions are of the form
  </p>

  <p><m>M(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m>, where <m>a, b, c ∈ ℝ</m>, <m>a ≠ 0</m>.</p>

  <p>
    Combining this knowledge and what we know about linear inequalities, we
    can represent general quadratic inequalities in the forms;
  </p>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p><m>ax^2 + bx + c &lt; w</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><m>ax^2 + bx + c &gt; w</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><m>ax^2 + bx + c ≤ w</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p><m>ax^2 + bx + c ≥ w</m>, where <m>w</m> is a constant.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    To find the solution to a quadratic inequality of any of the forms above:
  </p>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>First determine the value of <m>w</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Simplify the inequality so that one side is zero</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Factorise to determine the range of values for <m>x</m></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Represent it graphically to know the solution region</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <activity>
    <p>
      Activity 2.6 – Individual/Group Exploration of Quadratic Inequalities
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Brainstorm how you would solve the inequalities:</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>x^2 - 3x - 10 &gt; 0</m> and <m>-2x^2 + 5x ≤ 3</m>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Discuss multiple approaches (factorising, completing the square or
            using the quadratic formula).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Consider when the expression is equal to zero and how this can
            help determine the solution.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Draw separately the graph of each of the quadratic inequalities to
            understand the regions where the inequality is true or false.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Share your observations from the process with a peer.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </activity>

  <example>
    <p>
      Example 2.18 Find the solution to the inequality <m>x^2 - 2x - 15 &lt;
      0</m>.
    </p>

    <p><term>Step 1:</term> Factorise the left-hand side of the inequality</p>

    <p><m>x^2 - 2x - 15 = (x + 3)(x - 5)</m></p>

    <p>Hence <m>(x + 3)(x - 5) &lt; 0</m></p>

    <p><term>Step 2:</term> Find the critical values</p>

    <p><m>x = 5</m> and <m>x = -3</m></p>

    <p><term>Step 3:</term></p>

    <p>
      <term>Method 1:</term> Represent the extreme values of the expected
      solution on a number line and test them.
    </p>

    <!-- div center -->

    <!-- end div center -->

    <!-- div center -->

    <tabular>
      <col halign="left"/> <col halign="left"/> <col halign="center"/>
      <col halign="center"/> <col halign="center"/> <col halign="left"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><term>Possible Solution Regions</term></cell>
        <cell><term>Test Values</term></cell>
        <cell><m>\boldsymbol{x + 3}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boldsymbol{x - 5}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boldsymbol{(x + 3)(x - 5)}</m></cell>
        <cell><term>Sign</term></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>x &lt; -3</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-4</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-9</m></cell>
        <cell>9</cell>
        <cell>Positive</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>-3 &lt; x &lt; 5</m></cell>
        <cell>3</cell>
        <cell>6</cell>
        <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-12</m></cell>
        <cell>Negative</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>x &gt; 5</m></cell>
        <cell>6</cell>
        <cell>9</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>9</cell>
        <cell>Positive</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <!-- end div center -->

    <p>
      <term>Step 4:</term> Select the solution region that corresponds to the
      sign satisfying the inequality.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>x^2 - 2x - 15 &lt; 0</m> holds for only values of <m>x</m> for
      which the product of the factors is negative (less than zero), <m>-3
      &lt; x &lt; 5</m> is the solution to the inequality.
    </p>

    <p><term>OR</term></p>

    <p><term>Step 3:</term></p>

    <p><term>Method 2:</term> Graph the quadratic inequality</p>

    <!-- div center -->

    <!-- end div center -->

    <p>
      <term>Step 4:</term> Identify the portion of the graph that satisfies
      the inequality. In this case, the portion below the <m>x</m>-axis.
    </p>

    <!-- div center -->

    <!-- end div center -->

    <p>Thus <m>-3 &lt; x &lt; 5</m></p>
  </example>

  <example>
    <p>
      Example 2.19 Find the solution to the inequality <m>-5x^2 + 12x - 7 &gt;
      0</m>.
    </p>

    <p><term>Step 1:</term> Factorise the left-hand side of the inequality</p>

    <p><m>-5x^2 + 12x - 7 = (-5x + 7)(x - 1)</m></p>

    <p>Hence <m>(-5x + 7)(x - 1) &gt; 0</m></p>

    <p><term>Step 2:</term> Find the critical values</p>

    <p><m>x = \frac{7}{5}</m> and <m>x = 1</m>.</p>

    <p>
      <term>Step 3:</term> Represent the extreme values of the expected
      solution on a number line and test them.
    </p>

    <!-- div center -->

    <!-- end div center -->

    <!-- div center -->

    <tabular>
      <col halign="left"/> <col halign="left"/> <col halign="center"/>
      <col halign="center"/> <col halign="center"/> <col halign="left"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><term>Possible Solution Regions</term></cell>
        <cell><term>Test Values</term></cell>
        <cell><m>\boldsymbol{-5x + 7}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boldsymbol{x - 1}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\boldsymbol{(-5x + 7)(x - 1)}</m></cell>
        <cell><term>Sign</term></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>x &lt; 1</m></cell>
        <cell>0</cell>
        <cell>7</cell>
        <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>-7</m></cell>
        <cell>Negative</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1 &lt; x &lt; 1.4</m></cell>
        <cell>1.2</cell>
        <cell>1</cell>
        <cell>0.2</cell>
        <cell>0.2</cell>
        <cell>Positive</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>x &gt; 1.4</m></cell>
        <cell>3</cell>
        <cell><m>-8</m></cell>
        <cell>2</cell>
        <cell><m>-16</m></cell>
        <cell>Negative</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <!-- end div center -->

    <p>
      <term>Step 4:</term> Select the solution region that corresponds to the
      sign satisfying the inequality.
    </p>

    <p>
      Since <m>-5x^2 + 12x - 7 &gt; 0</m> holds for only values of <m>x</m>
      for which the product of the factors is positive (greater than zero),
      <m>1 &lt; x &lt; 1.4</m> is the solution to the inequality.
    </p>
  </example>

  <activity>
    <p>
      Activity 2.7 – Graphical Solution to <m>-5x^2 + 12x - 7 &gt; 0</m>
      Represent the solution to <m>-5x^2 + 12x - 7 &gt; 0</m> graphically and
      share the results with a classmate.
    </p>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <p>Activity 2.8 – Research on Quadratic Inequalities</p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Conduct research on the behaviour of solutions to maximum and
            minimum curves of quadratic functions.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p><term>Instructions:</term></p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Explore the nature of the solution when a quadratic function with
            a minimum curve is:
          </p>

          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>greater than zero</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>less than zero</p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Explore the nature of the solution when a quadratic function with
            a maximum curve is:
          </p>

          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>less than zero</p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>greater than zero</p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Include graphical representations to support your argument.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Consult open education resources, YouTube, textbooks, etc.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Provide references to the sources you consult and use.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </activity>
</section>
